            <ArticleTitle>Leiomyosarcoma of the inferior vena cava in a patient with double inferior vena cava.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>859.e1-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.avsg.2011.11.043</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Leiomyosarcoma of the inferior vena cava (IVC) is a rare tumor arising from its smooth muscle cells.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We report the case of a 38-year-old woman presenting with back pain and weight loss who was diagnosed with a 22-cm leiomyosarcoma of the right IVC and thrombosis of the left IVC. The patient is alive and free of recurrence a year after radical tumor resection with removal of the affected IVC, reconstruction with polytetrafluoroethylene prosthetic graft, and anastomosis of both right and left IVC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Leiomyosarcoma is a rare and aggressive tumor with a deceitful course. Radical surgical en bloc resection is the mainstay of treatment for IVC leiomyosarcomas. For an adequate restoration of venous return, complex vascular repair may be necessary.</AbstractText>
